WEBVTT

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:26.080
gentlemen, the picture you are about to see is called The Naked City. My name is

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.000
Mark Hellinger. I was in charge of his production, and I may

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.300
as well tell you frankly that it's a bit different from most films you've ever seen. It was written

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:41.180
by Albert Maltz and Malvin Wald, photographed by William Daniels, and directed by Jules

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:49.330
Daskin. As you see, we're flying over an island, a city, a particular city. And this

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:55.600
is a story of a number of people, and a story also of the city itself. It was not photographed

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:00.000
in a studio. Quite the contrary. Barry Fitzgerald, our

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:12.570
Howard Duff, Dorothy Hart, Don Taylor, Ted DeCorsier, and the other actors played out their roles on the streets, in the apartment houses, in the skyscrapers of New York itself.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:17.770
And along with them, a great many thousand New Yorkers played out their roles also.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.880
This is the city as it is.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:28.580
Hot summer pavements, the children at play, the buildings in their naked stone, the people without makeup.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:37.360
well let's begin our story this way it's one o'clock in the morning on a hot summer night

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:45.520
and this is the face of new york when it's asleep or as nearly asleep as any city ever

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:55.460
is bad hunting on wall street at night no bulls no bears no lambs a bank is a lonely

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:00.000
place at this hour and even a theater has lost its magic

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:01.190
A question.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:04.760
Do the machines in a factory ever need rest?

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.240
Does a ship ever feel tired?

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.860
Or is it only people who are so weary at night?

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:13.970
There is a pulse to a city.

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:17.200
And it never stops people.

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:21.840
Some people earn their bread at night.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:27.280
Sometimes I think this world is made up of nothing but dirty food.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:30.000
It's wonderful.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:35.160
working on a newspaper you meet such interesting people you put on a record

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:40.970
you take it off you put on another does anyone listen to this program except my

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:47.960
wife and while some people work others are rounding off an evening of

00:02:47.960 --> 00:03:00.000
relaxation and still another is at the close

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:13.210
of her life be a fool he's got to be sure lift her up what you're gonna do

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:28.160
night has worked its way toward dawn

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:32.850
Texas beef for New York markets.

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:36.390
Uncle Harry's letter gets an early start.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.200
Everything is usual.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:41.480
The morning routine.

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:49.370
And even this, too, can be called routine in a city of eight million people.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:04:00.000
I've done a lot of things, but I never changed.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.980
I don't know what I'm gonna say to God when my time comes.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:17.550
He's got a big heart, I'm told, but he don't like me.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:20.810
I thought you were off the liquor.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:24.800
Liquor is bad, weakens your character.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:26.880
How can I make it?

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.000
I don't know what you're going to say to God.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:45.270
a man like me trust a liar like you, you can't.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:56.850
- It's an hour later now, 6:00 AM.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:00.000
- Babies are eight o'clock babies, some babies

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.350
a seven o'clock. Why do you have to be a six o'clock, baby?

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:18.860
Good boy, Patty, no matter where you roam, don't forget your country or what you left

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:27.130
at home. Write your letter now and then send me all you can. But Patty, dear, don't forget

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:30.000
you are an Irish man.

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:33.520
And now it's time to go to work.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.440
Eat and run, buddy.

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:40.270
Goodbye, Mrs. Halloran.

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:41.390
Goodbye, Mr. Halloran.

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:44.340
Yeah, come on, you little Indian, on your horse.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:46.130
See you tonight, Mac.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:48.060
Bye.

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:53.620
Bye, kids.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:55.970
Gonna be a scorcher today.

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:59.280
Yeah, so next week I'll get my vacation that'll be freezing.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.760
She went to Jones' beach last night.

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:03.620
Had a picnic.

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:04.620
With her boyfriend?

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:05.400
Yeah.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:06.850
Did he get fresh again?

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:07.850
Yeah.

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:11.200
Gee, she was born with a silver spoon.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:21.540
For this woman, the day will not be ordinary.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:29.470
Martha Swenson, 42 years old.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.190
lives a quiet life as a house worker.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:03.700
The 10th Precinct Station is in the Chelsea District of New York.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:06.860
A rather shabby building on a rather shabby street.

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:11.780
Acts of violence in Manhattan are reported to the third floor of this building.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:16.320
Because here, rather quietly, the Homicide Squad does its work.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:18.830
Sometimes I wonder what the human heart is made out of.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:24.930
My wife, rest her soul, always said she'd rather look into a man's heart than into his head.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.500
That she could tell more about him.

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:30.000
Just came in, Captain.

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:32.180
- Morning, Mr. Irwin. - Hi, Dave.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:38.230
- You're free, aren't you, Dan? - Haven't had a hard day's work since yesterday.

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:40.920
- Woman drowned in the bathtub. - Who's to do my legwork?

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:44.060
- How about Chuck Halloran again? - All right. I like the boy.

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:46.940
- How's he doing? - Oh, he's making the same mistakes I made at his age.

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:51.960
That's too bad. I thought he showed promise.

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:02.600
Elevator straight ahead, Lieutenant.

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:03.140
Okay.

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:12.090
Who's on the job, then?

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:13.620
Sergeant Schaefer, 20th Squad.

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:17.790
Morning, Lieutenant.

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:18.430
Hey, Schaefer.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:19.880
Well, what's the story?

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:23.100
The dead woman's name was Jean Dexter, 26 years old, unmarried.

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:26.210
She used to be a dress model at Grace Hewitt's on West 57th Street.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:28.080
Her parents live in Lakewood, New Jersey.

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:29.310
Her name is Bittori.

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:30.000
That's Polk.

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:33.430
Her name used to be Mary Bittori until she came to New York.

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:35.670
The ambulance doctor said she died of drowning.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:39.030
- That's all I have. - Is this her?

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:46.230
Yeah. Martha Swenson, the woman's housekeeper. She found the body.

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:49.170
Mr. Harvey, he's the house superintendent. He called headquarters.

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:50.740
- Where's the body? - In there.

00:09:57.170 --> 00:10:00.000
- Didn't this woman drown the bathtub, Doctor? - She was on the bed when I got up.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.770
You come to work at the same time every day.

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:05.770
Every day except Thursday. That's my day off.

00:10:05.770 --> 00:10:06.770
Who moved the bottle?

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:14.030
When I came in and saw her like that in the tub, I called Mr. Harvey here. He helped me.

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:17.050
You should have waited for the police. Both of you should have known better.

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:18.050
I was so upset.

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:20.100
Dan. Say, Dan.

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:23.920
I found a bottle of pills under the bed. Looks like sleeping pills.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:24.870
Let me see them.

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:26.410
I left them there.

00:10:26.410 --> 00:10:30.000
Well, thank you for that, Jimmy. This is moving daily.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.080
around here. I thought maybe you caught the fever. About the pills. Maybe the dame took

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:41.310
an overdose. Jimmy, it's a obligation to wait for the medical examiner. He's a learned physician

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:46.410
employed by the city to determine the cause of mysterious deaths. Let the good man earn

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:57.510
his money. No accident. No suicide. Bruises on her throat, shoulders, and arms. Those slight

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:00.000
burns around her mouth and nose were caused by

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.820
She was anesthetized after a struggle, then dumped into the tub alive.

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:07.840
How do you know that, Doctor?

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:09.760
By the white foam around her mouth.

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:11.200
Proof she drowned.

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.270
Knew?

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:15.320
Knew.

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:16.230
Okay, Lieutenant.

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:17.370
Okay, Doctor.

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:18.200
Body's yours.

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:19.090
Start working, gentlemen.

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:22.470
Just smudges.

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:27.410
There!

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:29.830
There!

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:35.050
Men's pajamas.

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:36.500
Find them in the laundry hamper.

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:38.460
No visible laundry marks, no label.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:39.460
Now be a fancy.

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:41.290
You don't get these for $3.95.

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:43.540
Nick, pick up these pajamas on your way out.

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:47.670
I want them checked right down the line.

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:49.770
What time does the elevator boy come on in the morning?

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:50.570
7 o'clock.

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:53.510
Martha, who belongs to these?

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:55.320
Oh, I don't know, sir.

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:56.790
I'm so unstrung.

00:11:56.790 --> 00:11:59.190
I know you are, but I think you'd like to help us.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:00.000
Oh, I would.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.820
She was such a sweet girl, a little wild by my tenders maybe.

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:07.820
But live and let live, I say. She always treated me fine.

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:11.570
- With pajamas, Martha. - Oh, I'm all in pieces.

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:17.870
- Martha? - Well, they could belong to Mr. Henderson.

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:22.770
- What's Mr. Henderson's first name? - Philip, I think.

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:25.770
He lives in Baltimore, at least that's what she told me.

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:30.000
I only saw him once. All I know is he was an admirer.

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:33.030
I'm all in little pieces, what a nightmare.

00:12:33.030 --> 00:12:35.030
You're being a great help to us, Martha.

00:12:35.030 --> 00:12:36.890
How old would you say, Mr. Anderson is?

00:12:36.890 --> 00:12:39.770
Oh, fifty about.

00:12:39.770 --> 00:12:42.040
What does he look like?

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:44.190
Well, like I say, I only saw him once.

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:47.050
He was coming in just as I was going home.

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:49.970
He's quite tall, on the thin side.

00:12:49.970 --> 00:12:52.290
Anything else? Does he wear glasses?

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:55.440
Oh, no, no, that's all I can remember.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:57.440
Mm-hmm.

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:58.870
Oh, no, no, that's all I can remember.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:00.660
Do you know Henderson?

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:01.740
Never saw him.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:04.180
Shoot a wire on this to Baltimore.

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:08.250
Lieutenant, here's the ring she was wearing.

00:13:08.830 --> 00:13:10.150
I'll phone you after the autopsy.

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:11.270
Have fun.

00:13:11.450 --> 00:13:12.190
Always do.

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:21.520
Sir, that ring, it's a black star sapphire, very rare.

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:23.710
She said her brother sent it to her from India.

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:25.660
Did she have any other jewelry?

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:26.900
Oh, a lot.

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:27.920
Valuable.

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:30.000
She kept it in a jewel box locked.

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:32.880
- Let's go get her. - No.

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:39.990
- Please. - Okay, you can pick up that bottle under the bed now.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:42.170
- Check. - That one there.

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:45.780
- Nick, can we open a drawer on this table? - Yeah, I've gone over it.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:49.710
- What are you doing to the furniture? - Investigating it.

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:54.930
She had chamois bags full of bracelets and rings, diamond rings.

00:13:55.030 --> 00:13:57.380
They're gone. - Can you describe the jewelry?

00:13:57.480 --> 00:13:59.720
- Well, most of it, I think. - Fine.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.400
- Yes, sir. - Pretty little slumber pellets.

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:08.260
Jimmy, I want to go on questioning those two in there. You start your legwork.

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:12.330
Take the number of that prescription. See the druggist. From him, go to the doctor.

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:14.570
Then go to the dress shop she worked at.

00:14:14.570 --> 00:14:21.650
Right. Lieutenant, the newspaper men are here.

00:14:21.650 --> 00:14:25.240
Okay, I'm coming. Getting any fingerprints, Nick?

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:30.000
Nothing good so far. Fragmentary prints, smudges, that's all.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.920
- You mean like a heavy case? - A heavy case.

00:14:36.580 --> 00:14:39.190
An investigation for murder is now underway.

00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:42.480
It will advance methodically by trial and error...

00:14:42.630 --> 00:14:45.270
by asking a thousand questions to get one answer...

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:47.860
by brainwork and legwork.

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:51.670
When it comes to legwork, Detective Jimmy Halloran is an expert.

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:55.890
In the war, he walked halfway across Europe with a rifle in his hand.

00:14:56.530 --> 00:14:59.640
Up until three months ago, he was pounding a beat in the Bronx.

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:03.770
And now he's playing button-button in a city of eight million people.

00:15:04.820 --> 00:15:07.420
No, the druggist can't remember Mr. Dexter personally.

00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:09.310
He'll have to look up the prescription.

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:16.710
Here it is.

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:20.120
The doctor's name was Lawrence Stoneman, office in the Chafee Building.

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:24.790
The Chafee Building, Halloran, 18 blocks south, 4 blocks west.

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:29.140
Nick.

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:33.810
- How are you doing? - Too bad.

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:36.400
- I found these two hairs in the rug. - Mm-hmm.

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:39.420
- Getting any fingerprints? - Nothing good yet.

00:15:39.580 --> 00:15:40.780
Thanks.

00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:47.290
Martha, aside from Mr. Henderson, did Miss Dexter have any other men friends?

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:49.080
None that I know of, sir.

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:51.930
Just this Niles man, Frank Niles.

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:53.720
Oh, lovely man.

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:55.890
- What are you doing? - Don't mind me.

00:15:56.380 --> 00:15:58.100
Just admire your hair.

00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:23.440
- Is Dr. Stoneman in?

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:24.970
- You have an appointment?

00:16:24.970 --> 00:16:29.280
- I'm from the police department, quite important.

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:30.000
- Okay, please.

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:45.320
There's your city, Halloran. Take a good look.

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:49.780
Gene Dexter is dead. The answer must be somewhere down there.

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:52.840
Yes, sir. What can I do for you? Have a seat.

00:16:53.290 --> 00:16:56.570
Thank you. I want to ask you about a patient of yours.

00:16:57.080 --> 00:16:57.810
Gene Dexter.

00:16:58.380 --> 00:16:58.880
Dexter?

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:00.000
You were a prescription.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:07.260
for about two weeks ago. Sleeping pills. Yes, a blonde girl. Very handsome. I remember now.

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:12.700
Dexter, you from the local precinct officer? Homicide. Oh, don't tell me that girl murdered

00:17:12.700 --> 00:17:19.740
someone. Someone murdered her. What? When? Last night sometime. What do you want to know?

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:26.000
Well, whatever you can tell me about it. She needed a good spanking, took stimulants

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:30.000
by day and needed sleeping pills at night. I told her to go slow, but no, like

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:34.110
- Can you tell me anything about the way she lived?

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:35.630
Her friends?

00:17:35.630 --> 00:17:36.990
- No, nothing.

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:39.040
I saw her only that one visit.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:42.640
- I guess that's all, Doc.

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:43.620
- Thank you.

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:49.030
- The dress shop is next, Halloran.

00:17:49.030 --> 00:17:50.890
Grace Hewitt's on West 57th Street.

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:55.020
- Imagine me in that.

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:57.780
- I can't imagine.

00:17:57.780 --> 00:18:00.000
In the Waldorf Astor.

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:01.570
With Frankie singing.

00:18:02.170 --> 00:18:03.760
I can't imagine.

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:07.500
Oh, you. You're so uncooperative, I could slam you.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:13.240
And somewhere in the back of her pretty head was the fixed notion that she couldn't be happy without being rich.

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:16.760
I don't think Jean ever would have married unless the man had money.

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:17.780
Real money.

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:19.440
Why did you fire her?

00:18:20.570 --> 00:18:22.410
Gentlemen sometimes come here with their wives.

00:18:22.570 --> 00:18:26.400
When Jean Dexter modeled, many of them left my shop a little too interested in her.

00:18:27.370 --> 00:18:28.410
Their wives didn't like it.

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:29.640
And neither did I.

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:35.320
I see. Can I talk to her friend now, the model you spoke about?

00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:37.320
- Ruth Morrison? - Yes, I'll call her.

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:41.550
- It's getting late. We better go. - So what if we're late?

00:18:41.550 --> 00:18:44.550
- The boss will holler. - Let him holler. Strengthen his lungs.

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:48.130
Miss Morrison, I understand you modeled with Jean Dexter.

00:18:48.130 --> 00:18:50.950
Do you know anybody who has cause to dislike her?

00:18:50.950 --> 00:18:53.270
- No. - How about Mrs. Henderson?

00:18:53.270 --> 00:18:57.960
- Who's she? - Mr. Henderson and Miss Dexter are quite friendly, aren't they?

00:18:57.960 --> 00:19:00.000
She never told me of a man named Henderson.

00:19:00.090 --> 00:19:01.090
Are you sure?

00:19:01.090 --> 00:19:04.010
Really, Mr. Halloran, Jean's my friend.

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:07.010
I don't think I want to answer any more questions unless you tell me why you're asking them.

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:10.370
She was found dead this morning.

00:19:10.370 --> 00:19:16.930
Hey, look at the whale!

